Chapter: GopCornerRamp
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Works chapter GopDraw
  cites Works chapter GopComposite
  cites UI chapter Theme
  cites UI chapter Layout
  cites UI chapter BoxModel

 A rounded corner was antialiased one pixel per row, and the top of an arc is
 exactly where that is not enough. The inset is `r - sqrt(r^2 - (r-d)^2)`,
 whose slope is vertical at the top: at radius 13 the first row is inset by all
 13 pixels and the second by 8, so the shape moves five pixels sideways inside
 one row while one pixel gets a partial value. Damian, on a taskbar pill,
 2026-09-07: "it looks like shoulder pads on a football player ... which makes
 divits in the run".

 WHAT IS MEASURED IS THE RAMP, not the silhouette. A staircase and a smooth
 corner have the same fully-covered pixels; they differ in how many pixels are
 PARTLY covered and whether those partial values climb. So the top row is
 walked and its partial pixels are counted and checked for monotonicity. A
 count of one is the defect this file was written for.

 THE THREE STYLES MUST GIVE THREE DIFFERENT ANSWERS, and that is what makes
 this more than a count. CornerSharp has no arc and reports ZERO partial
 pixels. CornerBevel is a straight diagonal, which crosses exactly ONE pixel
 per row, and reports one. Only CornerRound reports a run, and only where the
 arc is shallow: five on the top row, two on the next, one by row six where
 the curve has turned steep, none on the straight sides. A change that simply
 blended everything everywhere would flatten those four numbers into one and
 fail here.

 THE BEVEL'S PIXEL WAS PREDICTED AS ZERO AND MEASURED AS ONE. The reasoning
 was that a bevel has no fractional part, which is true of its INSET and not
 of its coverage: the diagonal passes through the middle of the pixel it
 crosses, so half of one is exactly right and the old code drew it opaque. The
 prediction is recorded rather than quietly corrected because the measurement
 improved the control (L-SABOTAGE): three distinct answers discriminate where
 two would not have.

 THE SILHOUETTE IS REPORTED BESIDE THE RAMP so a smoother corner cannot be
 bought by eating another pixel of the box. The first fully covered pixel on
 the top row is 13, the radius, and it was 13 before this change.

Section: Geometry

  cr-w : Integer = 64

  cr-h : Integer = 32

  cr-stride : Integer = 64

  cr-cells : Integer = 2048

  cr-bg : Integer = 0

  cr-fg : Integer = 16777215

  cr-radius : Integer = 13

Section: Body

  cr-yn : Boolean -> Text
  cr-yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "NO"

  cr-fill : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cr-fill (b) (i) (n) (c) =
    if i >= n then 0 else let p = poke-32 b (i * 4) c in cr-fill b (i + 1) n c

 A fresh buffer for every arm, painted with the background first, because
 `comp-blend-px` reads the destination back and mixes: a reused buffer would
 blend a corner over a previous corner and the second reading would be a
 record of both.

  cr-render : CornerStyle -> Integer
  cr-render (cs) =
    let b = alloc-bytes (cr-cells * 4)
    in let z = cr-fill b 0 cr-cells cr-bg
    in let d = comp-rows b cr-stride cr-h (layout-rect 0 0 cr-w cr-h) (layout-rect 0 0 cr-w cr-h) cs cr-fg cr-fg False False 0 1
    in b

  cr-at : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cr-at (b) (x) (y) = peek-32 b ((y * cr-stride + x) * 4)

  cr-partial : Integer -> Boolean
  cr-partial (v) = v /= cr-bg & v /= cr-fg

 Counted across the LEFT half only. The right half is the mirror of it and
 counting both would double every number without asking a new question.

  cr-count : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cr-count (b) (y) (x) (acc) =
    if x >= cr-w / 2 then acc
    else cr-count b y (x + 1) (acc + (if cr-partial (cr-at b x y) then 1 else 0))

 The ramp must climb toward the middle of the box: a partial pixel further in
 is more covered than one further out. This walks the same half and refuses
 any pair that goes the wrong way, so a set of partial pixels carrying noise
 rather than a gradient fails here while passing the count.

  cr-climbs : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  cr-climbs (b) (y) (x) (last) =
    if x >= cr-w / 2 then True
    else let v = cr-at b x y
    in if cr-partial v == False then cr-climbs b y (x + 1) last
    else if v < last then False
    else cr-climbs b y (x + 1) v

 The solid run is where the shape is fully opaque, and it is reported so that
 a reader can see the silhouette did not move: a fix that smoothed the corner
 by eating another pixel of the box would show up here rather than passing
 quietly.

  cr-solid : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cr-solid (b) (y) (x) =
    if x >= cr-w then x
    else if cr-at b x y == cr-fg then x
    else cr-solid b y (x + 1)

  cr-row : Text, CornerStyle, Integer -> Text
  cr-row (nm) (cs) (y) =
    let b = cr-render cs
    in let n = cr-count b y 0 0
    in nm & " row " & integer-to-text y
       & ": partial pixels " & integer-to-text n
       & "  first solid at " & integer-to-text (cr-solid b y 0)
       & "  climbs " & cr-yn (cr-climbs b y 0 0)

 THE OUTLINE MUST FOLLOW THE ARC, which is a different claim from the ramp
 above and failed while the ramp passed. `comp-border` drew four straight bars
 whose ends stopped at the radius and never traced the corner, on the stated
 reasoning that a square outline reads as a rounded one. It does not when the
 outline is a colour of its own: the fill's arc reaches out past where the bar
 begins, leaving the bar ending in a square cut with bare fill beside it.

 The question is asked as "what is the FIRST thing you meet coming in from
 outside", row by row down the corner. On a bordered box the honest answer is
 the border, on every row, because the outline is the outside of the shape.
 Before the fix the top rows answered with the FILL, which is the defect
 stated as a measurement. The straight-sided rows below the corner are in the
 walk too, and they always answered border: without them a fix that painted
 the border everywhere would pass.

  cr-ground : Integer = 12632256

  cr-border-col : Integer = 4144959

  cr-fill-col : Integer = 8421504

 DRAWN AT THE SCALE THE DESK DRAWS AT, which is 2, and the reason is that a
 ONE pixel antialiased diagonal has no fully opaque pixel anywhere along it:
 every pixel of it is a blend, the walk below skips the lot, and a correct
 outline scores as absent. That is a property of the instrument and not of the
 outline. The desk's border is one LOGICAL pixel, which is two device pixels
 at this scale, and two is enough to leave an opaque core. The layout rect is
 halved to match, so the device box is the same 64 by 32 the rows above use.

  cr-sr : Integer = 6

  cr-styled : Integer
  cr-styled =
    let b = alloc-bytes (cr-cells * 4)
    in let z = cr-fill b 0 cr-cells cr-bg
    in let bdr = border-uniform 1 cr-border-col (CornerRound cr-sr)
    in let st = widget-style cr-fill-col cr-fill-col bdr edges-zero edges-zero
    in let d = comp-box b cr-stride cr-h (layout-rect 0 0 cr-w cr-h) (layout-rect 0 0 (cr-w / 2) (cr-h / 2)) st cr-fill-col 2
    in b

 BLENDS ARE SKIPPED AND THE FIRST OPAQUE COLOUR IS THE ANSWER. The traced arc
 is antialiased against the background, so the outermost pixel of the outline
 is a blend and matches neither colour exactly; asking "what is the first
 non-background pixel" therefore answered neither and scored a correct outline
 as wrong. What the eye is being asked about is which of the two SURFACES
 comes first, so the walk runs in until it meets one of them at full opacity.

  cr-first : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cr-first (b) (y) (x) =
    if x >= cr-w / 2 then 0 - 1
    else let v = cr-at b x y
    in if v == cr-border-col then cr-border-col
    else if v == cr-fill-col then cr-fill-col
    else cr-first b y (x + 1)

  cr-meets : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  cr-meets (b) (y) = cr-first b y 0 == cr-border-col

  cr-krows : Integer = 15

  cr-walk : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cr-walk (b) (y) (acc) =
    if y >= cr-krows then acc
    else cr-walk b (y + 1) (acc + (if cr-meets b y then 1 else 0))

  cr-outline : Text
  cr-outline =
    let b = cr-styled
    in let n = cr-walk b 0 0
    in "outline: rows meeting the border first " & integer-to-text n & " of " & integer-to-text cr-krows
       & "  every row " & cr-yn (n == cr-krows)
       & "  top row meets border " & cr-yn (cr-meets b 0)
       & "  a straight row meets border " & cr-yn (cr-meets b (cr-krows - 1))

 A SHADOW MUST NOT STRAND BARE GROUND BETWEEN ITSELF AND THE BOX. The shadow
 skipped an EXCLUSION RECTANGLE, on the sound reasoning that the fill is about
 to overwrite the box's interior anyway and blending under it would be paid
 for twice. The rectangle was square and the fill is round, so at each corner
 the pixels outside the arc but inside that square were skipped by the shadow
 and never reached by the fill: what showed was the surface underneath, in a
 square cornered patch around a round button. Damian: "the frame is still
 square behind it ... etching out the light gray fill on the gradient
 taskbar".

 The measurement is a hole count. Scanning a row inward, the shadow's outer
 edge is the first pixel that is not background; after that point every pixel
 belongs to the shadow, the border or the fill, so a ground pixel found AFTER
 it is ground that nothing painted. Measured at scale 2 over the corner rows:
 25 such pixels before this fix and 0 after. A square shadow behind a square
 box strands none either, which is why the sharp control is here: it says the
 counter can reach 0 honestly rather than only by the corner being absent.

 THE GROUND IS A LIGHT GREY AND NOT THE BLACK THE ROWS ABOVE USE, and that is
 not decoration. shadow-subtle 0 is a BLACK shadow; painted over a black
 ground it lands on the same value the counter calls untouched, so the walk
 could not tell shadow from bare ground, never set its flag, and reported 0
 holes for the broken code and the fixed one alike. The first version of this
 row did exactly that and passed vacuously.

  cr-holes-row : Integer, Integer, Integer, Boolean, Integer -> Integer
  cr-holes-row (b) (y) (x) (seen) (acc) =
    if x >= cr-w / 2 then acc
    else let v = cr-at b x y
    in if v == cr-ground then cr-holes-row b y (x + 1) seen (acc + (if seen then 1 else 0))
    else cr-holes-row b y (x + 1) True acc

  cr-holes : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cr-holes (b) (y) (acc) =
    if y >= cr-krows then acc
    else cr-holes b (y + 1) (acc + cr-holes-row b y 0 False 0)

  cr-shadowed : CornerStyle -> Integer
  cr-shadowed (cs) =
    let b = alloc-bytes (cr-cells * 4)
    in let z = cr-fill b 0 cr-cells cr-ground
    in let bdr = border-uniform 1 cr-border-col cs
    in let st = widget-style-rich cr-fill-col cr-fill-col bdr edges-zero (edges-uniform 2) (shadow-subtle 0) gradient-none accent-border-none
    in let d = comp-box b cr-stride cr-h (layout-rect 0 0 cr-w cr-h) (layout-rect 0 0 (cr-w / 2) (cr-h / 2)) st cr-fill-col 2
    in b

  cr-gap : Text
  cr-gap =
    let round = cr-holes (cr-shadowed (CornerRound cr-sr)) 0 0
    in let sharp = cr-holes (cr-shadowed CornerSharp) 0 0
    in "shadow: stranded background pixels, round " & integer-to-text round
       & "  sharp " & integer-to-text sharp
       & "  round strands none " & cr-yn (round == 0)
       & "  and sharp never did " & cr-yn (sharp == 0)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    act
      print-line-uni (cr-row "round " (CornerRound cr-radius) 0)
      print-line-uni (cr-row "round " (CornerRound cr-radius) 1)
      print-line-uni (cr-row "round " (CornerRound cr-radius) 6)
      print-line-uni (cr-row "round " (CornerRound cr-radius) 16)
      print-line-uni (cr-row "bevel " (CornerBevel cr-radius) 0)
      print-line-uni (cr-row "sharp " CornerSharp 0)
      print-line-uni cr-outline
      print-line-uni cr-gap
      print-line-uni "gop-corner-ramp: reached the end"
    end
